WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.510
- Welcome everyone.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.130
My name is Jeremy Schwartz.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.690
I'm an Associate Professor of Medicine

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:10.830
at the Yale School of Medicine

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.650
and of Epidemiology at the
Yale School of Public Health.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.020
I'm also the lead of the
Faculty Network Development

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.430
of Faculty Network Development

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.440
at the Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.850
I'm delighted to welcome you

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.090
to today's conversation series
event with Winnie Byanyima

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.520
the Executive Director of UNAIDS.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.140
I'm coming to you today from Uganda,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.100
where I'm currently living,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.650
and where I've been engaged
as a clinician, educator

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.960
and researcher for nearly 20 years.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.570
So the fact that Winnie
Byanyima, our guest is a Ugandan,

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.150
makes this even more special for me.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.650
In addition to YIGH,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.080
today's event is co-sponsored
by the Walker Fund

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.430
at the MacMillan Center

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.380
and the Center for
Interdisciplinary Research

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.050
on HIV and AIDS at Yale.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.020
It's a pleasure to be with
all of you here today.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.530
Many of the drivers of gender
inequality in the HIV epidemic

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.600
are the same as those driving inequality

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.880
in the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.630
For example, as we've seen
during the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.660
women and girls are at heightened
risk of domestic violence

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.180
and inadequate access
to essential healthcare,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.610
including HIV prevention and sexual

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.950
and reproductive health services.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.270
To better prepare for the future,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.680
during today's conversation,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.630
we will discuss the imperative

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.150
to address the underlying social factors,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.830
including gender inequality
that exacerbate these pandemics.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.230
These issues of equity
and social determinants

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.810
and their impact on health
at home and around the world

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.570
are important for Yale
faculty and students.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:34.920
The faculty network program

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.690
at the Yale Institute for Global Health

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.840
is proud to support
almost a dozen networks

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.550
that address some of
the greatest challenges

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:42.900
in global health.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.790
You're welcome to learn
more about our program

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.323
on the YIGH website.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.750
In tandem with this event today

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.700
and in close collaboration with CIRA,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.879
the Center for Interdisciplinary
Research on AIDS at Yale,

00:01:53.879 --> 00:01:58.110
we launch the newest addition
to our faculty network program

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.140
known as the Yale Global
HIV/AIDS Research Network

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.060
or GARNER for short.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.380
This new network will aim

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.990
to bring together Yale
faculty and other partners

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.660
to support global
interdisciplinary research

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.730
on the implementation of HIV prevention

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.994
and treatment services
that reduce disparities

00:02:14.994 --> 00:02:17.583
and work toward ending the HIV epidemic.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.950
To introduce Winnie Byanyima

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.080
and to moderate today's conversation,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.990
I'll now turn this over to my
colleague, mentor and friend,

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.713
and one of the leads of
GARNER, Dr. Luke Davis.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.130
- Thank you very much, Jeremy.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:32.963
Hello everyone.

00:02:32.963 --> 00:02:35.040
My name is Luke Davis and
I'm Associate Professor

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.080
of Epidemiology and of Medicine at Yale.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.340
I'm also the faculty lead

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.900
of the International Research Program,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.620
the Center for Research on AIDS at Yale.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.990
And I'm a clinician and researcher

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.140
who's been working in
Uganda for about 17 years

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.190
as part of a collaborations

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.310
at Makerere University on TB and HIV.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.000
On December 1st, we will
commemorate World AIDS Day,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.370
a day when we'll reflect
and renew our commitment

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.650
to ending the HIV epidemic.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.990
Four decades into the HIV response,

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.990
inequality still persists
for the most basic services

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.930
like testing, treatment and condoms,

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.330
and even more so for new technologies.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:13.860
Young women in Africa remain

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.010
disproportionately affected by HIV,

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.960
while coverage of dedicated programs

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.150
for them remains too low.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.303
We see similar issues in
the response to COVID-19.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.620
The theme for World AIDS
Day this year is equalize,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.660
where we will recognize the inequalities

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.210
which perpetuate the AIDS pandemic

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.703
and make sure that they
are not inevitable.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.230
This year, World AIDS Day
will be a call to action,

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.250
a push for all of us to work

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.410
for the proven programs and strategies

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.130
that are needed to address
inequalities and help end AIDS.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.140
With that in mind, it's
my distinct pleasure

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.920
to introduce today's distinguished
guest, Winnie Byanyima,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.963
the Executive Director of UNAIDS,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.780
a passionate and longstanding champion

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.240
of social justice and gender equality.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.530
Winnie leads UN's efforts to
end the AIDS epidemic by 2030.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.110
She believes that
healthcare is a human right

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.450
and was an early champion
of a people's vaccine

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.880
against the coronavirus that is available

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.300
and free of charge to everyone everywhere.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.330
Before joining UNAIDS, she served

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.030
as the Executive Director
of Oxfam International,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.920
empowering people to create a future

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.760
that is secure, just
and free from poverty.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.440
Winnie was elected for three terms

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.300
and served 11 years in the parliament

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.923
of her home country, Uganda.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.410
Winnie, also led the establishment

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.660
of the African Union
Commission's Directorate

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.220
of Gender and Development,

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.410
and also served as Director
of Gender and Development

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.570
at the UN Development Program.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.030
She founded the Forum
for Women in Democracy,

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.540
an influential Ugandan
non-governmental organization,

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.700
and it's been deeply
involved in building global

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.450
and African coalitions
on social justice issues.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.180
She's a passion advocate
and we're incredibly honored

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.860
to have her here with us today.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.450
Welcome, Winnie.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:04.530
- Professor Luke Davis
and Professor Schwartz,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.850
thank you for inviting me to
be part of this discussion.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.890
And you have to tell me,

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.810
because I'm not smart enough to know

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.890
how you managed to be
working in the sunshine

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.470
by Lake Victoria, and I'm sitting here

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.203
in dump and cold, Geneva.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:24.993
Gotta show me how you do that.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.320
Thank you very much for having me,

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.040
and I'm looking forward to an exchange

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:36.720
with young people at Yale, is a privilege.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.463
How do you want this to go?

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:40.500
- Well, thanks Winnie.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.060
It's wonderful to have you with us.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.800
The theme of World AIDS Day
today of 2022 is equalized.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.140
And I wanted to start just by asking you,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.690
because you have been a
champion for this topic,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:56.220
why is equality so critical to
ending the HIV/AIDS epidemic?

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.950
And maybe more broadly, what
do you think is required

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.543
to achieve equality?

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.743
- Thank you for that question.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:10.610
I keep saying that
HIV/AIDS is an injustice

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.080
because today we have tools for prevention

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.860
and we have tools for testing

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:24.300
and tools for treatment.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.050
We don't have a vaccine
and we don't have a cure.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:33.050
But what we have in between
these tools that we have

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:38.190
of every type that will work for children,

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.833
will work for adults,
will work for gay people,

00:06:41.738 --> 00:06:46.020
for all groups of people
that these tools are there.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.020
But yet, last year we had
1.5 million new infections,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.950
most of them in Africa,

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:00.390
and we had 650,000 people

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:03.270
who died of AIDS related illnesses.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:08.040
So in spite of what we
have, what science provides,

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.660
we still have people nearly
infected, people dying.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:17.100
And when you peel that and
see who is being infected,

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.810
who is dying, that's when you really see

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:25.500
inequalities driving HIV/AIDS.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.850
As indeed they drive other
pandemics, as we saw in COVID,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.740
as you rightly put it.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.630
Take Africa...

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:38.160
And first inequalities of every kind.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.270
Gender inequalities, income inequalities,

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.220
racial inequalities.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.800
And often where they intersect

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.313
is where you have the
highest vulnerability.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:55.977
So I can give you some examples in Africa.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.583
In sub-Saharan Africa,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.340
adolescent girls and young
women are three times

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:10.340
as likely to acquire HIV as
adolescent boys and young men.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:16.683
That's just looking at a
gender inequality there.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.650
You also find between,

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.160
in the cohort of 15 and 19 years of age,

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:28.160
teenagers that six out of
seven newly infected teenagers

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.213
are girls.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.540
One is a boy, six are girls.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.350
It tells you the higher
vulnerability of girls.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.373
And why is that?

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.660
It's driven by gender inequalities.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.720
Unequal opportunity to education,

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.590
unequal opportunity to sexual information,

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:54.590
unequal access to services
and sexual violence,

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:02.390
which is actually a result
of a gender inequality.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:07.140
Sexual violence is an
indicator of gender inequality.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.620
So you find that inequalities are driving

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.210
the higher vulnerability

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.640
of adolescent girls and young women.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.640
4,200 adolescent girls young women

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:23.760
are infected every...

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.620
You might say every year.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:26.453
No.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.133
Every month?

00:09:28.133 --> 00:09:29.100
No.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:33.210
Every week, 4,200, it's a crisis.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.990
So then still talking about inequalities,

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.690
take the United States where you are,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.703
where many of the students are.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.780
Black people are eight and a half times

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:50.780
more likely to be diagnosed
with HIV than white people.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:55.380
That's just taking a racial difference.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.423
But if you take it further,

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:04.400
you'll find that black gay men

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:10.583
in the United States
have a one in two risk

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:17.210
of contracting HIV across
their lifetime, one in two.

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:21.693
Black gay men in the
richest country on earth.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:27.560
This is a higher lifetime risk
than the average in Uganda,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.400
low income country where I come from.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.570
And almost as high as the
risk in hotspot regions

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:39.333
like South Africa and Zimbabwe.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:41.760
Think about it.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.093
When two access of
inequality come together,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:51.540
sexual orientation and race,

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:55.470
you see the vulnerability,

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.660
the risk rising much higher.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:01.410
So inequalities, I could
give you more examples.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.940
In Canada, indigenous
people are several times

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:09.150
more vulnerable than the
rest of the population

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:10.620
and so on and so on.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.030
So we see...

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.940
And I have many good examples, but time.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:19.940
So we see that inequalities,
denial of opportunities,

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.880
denial of services,
unequal access to services,

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.310
discrimination and stigma,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:33.720
which result in inequality of opportunity

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:38.720
and inequality and access
to services or drive HIV.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:41.640
Close them, reduce them

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:46.640
and then you see vulnerability
and risk dropping very much.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.703
I'll give you one last example.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:58.351
In Malaysia where there's
a concentrated epidemic

00:11:58.351 --> 00:12:02.763
where same sex relations are criminalized,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:07.680
men who have sex with men are 72 times,

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.680
72 times, more likely
to be living with HIV

00:12:13.083 --> 00:12:15.030
then the general population.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:20.030
Not 72 times, 72% more
likely to be living with HIV

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:22.830
than the whole population.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.740
Now you compare that with Thailand,

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.770
another concentrated epidemic

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:33.570
where same sex relations
are not criminalized.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:38.010
Men who have sex with men are nearly 12%

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.680
more likely to be living with HIV.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.990
72, 12.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:47.990
So just removing the law
that hunts down gay men

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.280
reduces their vulnerability.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.820
So that is why we say equalize, equalize.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.060
'Cause when you equalize,
when you close the gaps,

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.840
you reduce the risk for others.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.150
And it also goes for deaths.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:10.410
because deaths are a result
of not staying on treatment.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.380
And when you have full access to treatment

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:16.830
and you have a equal right to treatment,

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:20.760
then again the risk of dying is less.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.890
So close the gaps,

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:27.780
close the gaps by removing
those laws that discriminate,

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.780
that drive stigma, equalize
opportunities of education,

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.860
of information, of access to healthcare,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.110
of social protection.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:45.110
And then you will reduce the
risk and also the deaths.

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:47.243
A long answer.

00:13:50.035 --> 00:13:50.970
- Thank you, Winnie.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.920
We know that at this time of year

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.170
we're thinking about the
global HIV/AIDS epidemic,

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.240
but of course it's many
different epidemics

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:58.710
affecting many different people.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:13:59.910
And I think you've highlighted

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:02.940
some of the unique barriers to delivery

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.610
of some of the gains of science

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.800
that have been great in
the field of HIV/AIDS,

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:12.360
but also gains in giving access to people.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.380
We need to go further in giving
equitable access to everyone

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.353
to these life saving interventions.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.160
I guess as you talk
about some of the drivers

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.560
some of the drivers are
similar around the world.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:31.110
Stigma may vary based on the population,

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:33.480
but we know stigma and discrimination,

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.490
whether it's adolescent
girls and young women

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:38.800
as you were talking
about, or black gay men

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:41.670
these are important factors.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.530
I guess another factor that I'd love

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.730
to hear you talk a little bit about

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.280
are economic inequalities.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.170
You mentioned that in your
first set of comments,

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:50.880
and I think we're all familiar

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:54.240
that we're facing a growing
worldwide economic crisis,

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.550
some related to the recent COVID pandemic

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:01.050
and also to other factors,
war in Ukraine, et cetera.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.150
And this has had major
effects on societies

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.100
around the world in 2022,

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:06.570
I know that you often write

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:09.900
and speak about how such economic factors

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.000
whether we're talking about inflation

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:16.170
or more specific issues like
global vaccine distribution,

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.890
how these can drive health
inequities and inequalities.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.790
And I'm just curious, given
that you've been very outspoken

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:26.370
and a strong advocate,
how do your perspectives

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:29.550
on economic justice influence your work

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.053
in your role at UNAIDS?

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.940
- Luke, I'm glad you raised that question

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:37.593
and I think here at UNAIDS,

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:44.930
our approach to fighting HIV/AIDS

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:49.970
has been leaning very heavily
on the biomedical side,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:55.500
less on looking at the
social determinants,

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:58.980
the structural barriers
that need to be removed.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:03.980
So we do not even have
sufficient data that we would use

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:11.100
to make this linkage
of economic inequality

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.010
and vulnerability to HIV.

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:17.250
But slowly we are pulling it together.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:22.250
For example, we know that
overall people in urban areas

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:27.830
have significantly higher
HIV prevalence than rural,

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:33.110
but that the urban poor
have the highest prevalence

00:16:33.322 --> 00:16:38.322
of all in country after
country, urban poor.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.500
We also know that in Namibia,

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:45.500
while 8% of richer people,
non poor, if you could say so

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.470
in cities are living with HIV.

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:54.470
15% of the urban poor
have HIV, at last measure.

00:16:55.680 --> 00:17:00.680
So we see a correlation
between income inequality

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:05.790
as well as all the social
factors that come with poverty

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:07.350
and higher vulnerability.

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:12.243
But now if you also look
at who gets services,

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:20.460
you see them skewed
towards richer populations,

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:25.460
towards urban populations
and less to rural poor.

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:30.690
That is also, you see that in
terms of access to services,

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:34.110
who is falling out of
treatment, for example.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:37.680
So in terms of economic injustices,

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:42.150
we see this also at the national level.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:45.540
At the national level, you see countries

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.523
that have the highest burden,

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:53.540
highest burden of HIV
struggling to pay debt.

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:59.910
And that debt keeps increasing

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:04.200
and taking more and more
out of the total budget

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:09.200
and squeezing out expenditures on health,

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:11.970
expenditures on education.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:16.230
Let me tell you what really
shocked me most was that

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:20.853
in the midst of the
crisis, the COVID crisis,

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:26.220
when countries that are
rich were powering trillion,

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:30.060
17 trillion was put into
the recovery cushioning,

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:32.190
the economy of rich countries.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:34.170
That's not talking about the money

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:38.820
put in to buying the COVID vaccine.

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:43.650
Developing countries, middle
income and low income,

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:48.650
where continuing to make debt repayments

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.940
that were four times, several times,

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:58.113
sometimes four times more
than their health budgets,

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:00.300
imagine that.

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:04.560
In a time of illness where
there was a killer disease

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:07.920
plus other diseases like HIV,

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:12.920
developing countries spent
27.5 of their total budget,

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:17.790
these are the poor countries
on debt repayments alone,

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:21.120
about a third on debt repayments.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:24.933
That as I said, four times
more than the health spent,

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:28.557
two times more than the education spent

00:19:28.557 --> 00:19:33.000
and 12 times more than social
prediction expenditure.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:34.740
This is an injustice.

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:39.000
In times of crisis even
you are at a family level

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:40.740
when you are in a mega crisis,

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:43.770
you go to the bank and
you reschedule your debt,

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:47.100
you're given a breathing
space so that you recover

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:48.480
and then you can pay.

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:50.730
But this was not possible.

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:53.760
So we saw at the same time...

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.110
By the way, we calculated that every day

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.680
the rich 20 countries
who are sitting right now

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:06.680
receive a 136 million per
day in debt repayments

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:12.390
from poor countries who are struggling

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:17.340
just to keep services
going in the health sector.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:18.720
So this is an injustice.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:21.330
We do not have a global mechanism,

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:24.000
international financial mechanism

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:28.803
for cushioning countries in
times of crisis like pandemics.

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:33.090
The IMF was created after
the second World War

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:35.250
for the purpose of cushioning countries

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:37.683
from economic and other shocks.

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:39.960
But look,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:43.410
when COVID hit and entire health sectors

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:46.983
were stretched completely,
healthcare systems,

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:52.110
they came together at the
IMF, they created new money,

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:55.110
which they call special drawing rights,

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:58.590
but they distributed it
according to the formula

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:02.460
that exists, which is that
the bigger shareholders

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:03.690
get the bigger share.

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:08.370
So they allocated the new
money to the rich ones

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:10.350
who didn't need it.

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:12.090
It was a scandal.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:14.310
There was a lot of
pressure from many of us

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:16.860
saying this is not meaningful.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:19.920
The ones who need most
this system of cushioning

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.900
isn't cushioning them.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:24.960
They came back now and said, okay

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.840
we shall take our new money

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:32.610
and we will loan it to you
at a concessionary rate.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.390
Imagine SDLs were
created to give you money

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:38.700
without incurring debt,

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:40.230
but now they are offering them

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:44.220
to the low income countries with a debt.

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:49.220
So there isn't a system to
cushion countries from pandemics,

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:51.780
and that's really an injustice.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:54.570
Healthcare should not be something

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:59.433
that waits for other
deals to, this is life.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.950
The second example is
about access to medicines.

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:07.020
I don't have to repeat the story

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:12.013
of what happened during COVID,
where a handful of companies,

00:22:12.013 --> 00:22:16.770
a handful were allowed to decide

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:19.200
how much vaccine is produced,

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.353
at what price it is
sold and who can buy it.

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:25.950
Poor countries pulled
their money together,

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:29.310
Africa tried to pull money together.

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:32.280
They were told stand in
the back of the queue

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.400
where richer countries took everything,

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.440
stocked up for themselves,

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.543
hoarded and even expired,

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:45.330
vaccines expired and this happened

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.340
because it is allowed happen

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:50.670
because the trade rules are now medicines,

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:55.297
life saving health commodities to be sold

00:22:56.190 --> 00:23:00.930
the way you can sell a luxury item,

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:03.780
like a fancy pen I'm holding here.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:05.700
How can that be?

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:06.870
But it's allowed.

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:10.680
We went to the trips, the WTO body

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:14.850
that regulates intellectual
property to ask

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:19.440
that there is a waiver
that allows all countries

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.013
to take that technology and
produce it for themselves.

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:27.300
No, it was not allowed and who refused?

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.994
A handful of countries whose companies

00:23:29.994 --> 00:23:32.430
were reaping the billions.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:35.720
So we saw Pfizer make 137 billion

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.510
in one year alone of profit

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:44.640
from a drug that had been produced

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:46.890
with the help of public resources

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:51.003
from you American taxpayers
and German taxpayers.

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:56.310
And we did an opinion poll.

00:23:56.310 --> 00:24:01.310
Ordinary Americans, Germans,
British people were saying,

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:05.200
no profit should be
made from this vaccines

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:10.020
before the COVID pandemic is over.

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:13.470
But the companies held the monopoly.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:17.610
So we argue against these trade rules

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:20.820
that treat life saving treatments

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:25.820
the same way they treat luxury
items like a Gucci handbag.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:29.100
We argue against that, it's an injustice.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:30.420
So these are the things

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:33.450
that are driving ill-health globally.

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:37.290
We need to change the
global rules of trade.

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:40.713
We need also to deal with
the rules of taxation.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:44.640
These companies are not paying

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.440
their fair share of taxes.

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:51.390
They are able to hide
through many tax loopholes,

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:54.660
to channel their profits
from one country to another,

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:58.320
put it in tax savings
where it's not taxed.

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:02.730
And that denies small
countries, poor countries,

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:04.500
the resources they need to pay

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.900
for the health of their people.

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:10.170
We've calculated the
billions that are transferred

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:14.550
to tax havens every year that
developing countries lose.

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:15.990
It's phenomenal.

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:20.990
It is several times over the
little aid that is received.

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:25.500
So sometimes people make
health an issue of aid.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:26.790
Yes, I'm sitting here

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:30.360
using an aid check from rich countries,

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:32.370
but it shouldn't be like that

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:35.940
if we corrected the tax injustices

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:38.850
in the global corporate tax system.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:43.850
So you see that global rules
enable or drive inequalities,

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:49.020
which result in ill health
of some, death of some

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:54.020
and huge mega profits for
a handful, for few people.

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:57.600
So that is what we mean
about the economic injustices

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:02.280
in the global system, but
also at the national level.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.260
We have our own governance

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:09.260
that are not prioritizing
everybody's health.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:14.780
In much of Africa, probably
more than 33 countries,

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:19.860
health is paid for by the poorest people,

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:24.860
user fees are collected for people to get,

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:28.830
and many times people die of malaria,

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:32.867
babies die of diarrhea because a mother

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:42.030
or a father can't afford
this small user fee charge.

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:43.710
And why is that?

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:48.240
You see money being channeled
through to other sectors,

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:52.470
to large public administrations
that you don't need,

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:55.020
to defense expenditures,

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:57.570
to all kinds of expenditures

00:26:57.570 --> 00:26:59.820
that are less important
than people's lives,

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:02.010
so that's an injustice.

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:07.010
So we see how economies,
the way they are managed,

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:09.300
the rules in the trade system,

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:14.300
in the taxation system,
the rules in technology,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:19.440
how technology is produced,
intellectual property rules

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:23.007
all these work against justice

00:27:23.007 --> 00:27:26.343
and drive pandemics such as HIV.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:30.090
I should stop there.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:32.520
- Thank you Winnie for highlighting...

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:35.700
I think the audience is chiming in

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:38.460
and appreciating your examples,

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:41.160
helping us understand sort
of this issue of equality

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:43.680
and I think particularly
the economic examples

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:47.070
that the structural factors
that are baked into our system

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:48.690
at the macroeconomic level.

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:50.850
You talked about some of
the microeconomic challenges

00:27:50.850 --> 00:27:52.560
that individuals face.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:54.840
And it's very, I think,

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:56.880
helpful to know what the barriers are.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:00.210
And you've also provided some solutions.

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:02.522
This is a conversation
with Winnie Byanyima

00:28:02.522 --> 00:28:04.890
and I encourage those in the audience

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.740
to add your questions into the chat.

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:10.200
We will get to a point in a few minutes

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:11.640
where we'll be taking those.

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:14.730
But I do have a few more questions
that I would love to ask.

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:16.130
And one of the factors--

00:28:16.130 --> 00:28:16.963
- I know you Luke

00:28:16.963 --> 00:28:18.840
I just want to give you one more example

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:22.290
because this is close to home.
- Yes please.

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:27.290
- Pharmaceutical companies in
the United States of America

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:33.060
paid more than a hundred,

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:37.990
more than a hundred million
dollars in lobbying fees,

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:46.160
lobbying your Congress and Senate.

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:47.693
Why?

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:53.430
It is not because they
are paying legislators

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:56.070
to find a cure for malaria.

00:28:56.070 --> 00:29:00.390
No, it is so that they can keep

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:02.913
the price of medicines high.

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:06.210
This is part of the problem.

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:09.360
The corporate grid and the
rules I was talking about

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:14.360
in the national systems that
allow certain things to happen,

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:19.830
lobbying by pharmaceutical companies

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:24.180
to keep prices high, to keep taxes low,

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:27.720
hurts ordinary citizens,

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:31.470
hurts not only ordinary
citizens in the United States,

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:33.870
but all over the world
because they are lobbying

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:37.710
about prices of goods
that they're going to sell

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.840
also to other countries.

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:43.320
So these are rules that have to change

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:45.420
if people have to have a chance

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:50.420
to access medicine that is
cheap, that is affordable.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:55.140
Sorry, I wanted to give
that example of lobbying

00:29:55.140 --> 00:30:00.140
as a rule that hurts the health of people.

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:03.570
- Enormous amount of money directed

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:07.410
to something that is
working against the efforts

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:10.590
that all of us are focused on.

00:30:10.590 --> 00:30:12.900
I'm curious the numbers of people

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:16.080
displaced by war and economic
crisis and natural disasters

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:18.960
is a topic that of special interest

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:21.690
to a number of researchers here at Yale

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:24.810
and clinicians as well and these numbers

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.610
have kinda climbed over the last decade.

00:30:26.610 --> 00:30:29.610
We know that women and girls comprise

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:33.060
more than half of refugee and
displaced people worldwide

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:36.060
and they're also in that
context at a greater risk

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:37.170
of gender based violence.

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:40.800
And I'm just curious,
given your perspective,

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.420
I know you've worked in the area

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:45.150
of humanitarian crisis
in your previous roles

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:46.650
and understand it well.

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:48.990
What can the global health community do

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:53.190
to support displaced
populations, refugee populations,

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:55.920
populations living in humanitarian crises

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.810
that are increasing around the world

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:00.270
so that we can optimize
the HIV/AIDS response

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:02.433
in these sort of emergency type settings?

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:08.040
- Crisis are increasing,

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:11.400
these numbers of displaced are increasing

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:14.910
because of the complexity of issues

00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:19.293
of war, climate crisis, poverty.

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:23.610
So people on the move are increasing

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:27.120
and those who are displaced
are also increasing.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:32.120
We have to find ways to get
health services to reach them.

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:38.330
And the surest way we know from experience

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:43.580
of working on HIV/AIDS
is to support communities

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:47.760
to deliver services for themselves.

00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:51.750
We found this to work, say in Ukraine.

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:55.950
Ukraine is a country

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:58.530
that has the second highest burden

00:31:58.530 --> 00:32:02.250
of HIV/AIDS in Eastern Europe,

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:05.253
Eastern Europe and
Central Asia, that region.

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:09.410
It's second into Russia
in terms of numbers

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:11.163
of people living with HIV.

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:15.990
Many are people who inject drugs

00:32:15.990 --> 00:32:17.913
or men who have sex with men.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:22.890
But Ukraine, unlike Russia

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:26.950
and many of the other
countries of Eastern Europe

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:32.400
had a system of delivering services

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:35.610
for HIV prevention and treatment

00:32:35.610 --> 00:32:38.340
that were driven by communities,

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:41.550
Ukrainian communities to lead services.

00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:44.250
So you would have gay communities

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:47.550
serve delivering services to gay people.

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:50.220
You have people who inject drugs,

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:53.430
trained to serve people who inject drugs.

00:32:53.430 --> 00:32:58.430
And you have geographical
communities serving themselves.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:04.790
Now, when the war broke out,
it was possible for many of us

00:33:04.917 --> 00:33:09.600
who were in global health
to follow these communities

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:11.220
and continue to support them

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:13.920
because they were moving with their people

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:16.890
and they knew their people
and they could find them.

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:21.090
And most importantly, for
a person living with HIV

00:33:21.090 --> 00:33:24.150
who say crosses from Ukraine to Moldova

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:26.613
or from one part of Ukraine to another,

00:33:27.914 --> 00:33:28.865
the first is it...

00:33:28.865 --> 00:33:30.900
This is not the first thing you want to do

00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:34.740
to identify yourself
as, I'm living with HIV.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:38.640
No, it's your secret, you fear stigma.

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:41.160
But when you are fling with your community

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:45.480
and your community leaders
who run the service

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:49.290
are looking for you,
have your cell number,

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:50.520
they'll find you

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:53.070
and you'll be able to
get on your treatment.

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:57.930
So we found that it was quick
for us in a couple of months.

00:33:57.930 --> 00:34:00.180
First they were cut cutoff from services,

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:03.210
but in a few months they
were back on services

00:34:03.210 --> 00:34:06.390
because of communities leading.

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:09.810
So for me, the biggest
solution is of course

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:12.870
to empower communities to lead services,

00:34:12.870 --> 00:34:16.890
they have the resilience to
adapt when things go bad.

00:34:16.890 --> 00:34:20.553
But secondly of course is
put the resources down,

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:24.600
to put down the resources that are needed

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:27.420
to reach people in trouble.

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:31.200
This humanitarian spirit is so important

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:36.150
and I think that this is
how our humanity is tested

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:38.160
to be there to save lives.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:42.240
And the last one is to
solve the root causes,

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:46.860
the conflicts, the climate crisis.

00:34:46.860 --> 00:34:48.510
Stop roasting our planet.

00:34:48.510 --> 00:34:50.793
We've got to stop the displacement.

00:34:52.106 --> 00:34:54.780
Back to you.

00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:55.613
- Thank you, Winnie,

00:34:55.613 --> 00:34:58.220
I really appreciate your highlighting

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:01.230
the power of pure lead
interventions in so many ways.

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:02.700
I think there's a number of examples

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:06.000
in our HIV/AIDS community here at Yale,

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:09.420
specifically with the respect
to the war in Ukraine,

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:12.870
responding to the needs
of people who inject drugs

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:15.090
and their need for opiate agonist therapy,

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:17.070
supporting people in HIV care.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:19.740
And then just responding more broadly.

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:22.710
Many in the Ukrainian
and Russian communities

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:23.823
here have responded.

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:26.190
And I know there's other examples,

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:28.470
we've seen it in Uganda as well.

00:35:28.470 --> 00:35:31.240
I wanted to ask you a bit about

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:34.230
given the audience that we have here

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:38.880
and I think the forward
looking view of World AIDS Day,

00:35:38.880 --> 00:35:41.400
what are your comments
about how we might educate

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:43.950
and train the next generation of students,

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:48.390
researchers, providers,
advocates and community members,

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:50.220
kind of highlighting
what you had said there

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:52.380
about peer interventions.

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:54.090
How can we harness this group

00:35:54.090 --> 00:35:57.090
and help them contribute best

00:35:57.090 --> 00:35:59.910
to meaningfully addressing inequalities

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:01.110
as we're focusing on today

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:03.423
and improving the global
HIV/AIDS response?

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:08.790
- That's a very hard one
'cause I'm not a professor,

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:10.443
I don't know how to teach.

00:36:11.608 --> 00:36:14.913
But let me think about young people.

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:17.613
I think that first of all,

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:25.470
young people are today not getting

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:30.470
as much information about
HIV/AIDS as an older generation.

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:37.320
And secondly, young people,
particularly in Africa,

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:40.830
but I also see it in other continents,

00:36:40.830 --> 00:36:45.750
are not getting sexuality education,

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:48.240
which is critical for life.

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:51.030
Young people are going through school

00:36:51.030 --> 00:36:54.810
without knowing about their
bodies, especially girls,

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:59.810
and knowing about how
to live a healthy life

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:05.043
and how to handle their sexuality
as they become teenagers.

00:37:06.420 --> 00:37:11.420
Young boys grow up with
toxic masculinities.

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:17.010
They understand their
gender in a violent way

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:26.690
and do not have a healthy,
peaceful masculinity.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:33.230
So there's a need for sexuality
education for boys and girls

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:38.580
in all our schools so that boys grow up

00:37:38.580 --> 00:37:41.610
with a healthy notion of masculinity

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:46.610
and also a positive attitude
towards health seeking.

00:37:47.010 --> 00:37:50.700
And girls also need to be
empowered about their bodies

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:53.550
and to know how to own their body

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:55.740
and make choices for themselves.

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.280
These things aren't happening

00:37:58.898 --> 00:38:01.170
and lives are being lost of young people.

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:06.003
In terms of, at a higher level,

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:12.200
I think that we need the
space, the academic freedom

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:18.500
to use it to discuss difficult
issues like human rights,

00:38:21.960 --> 00:38:24.450
LGBTQ rights.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:28.753
In a country where LGBTQ
people are criminalized,

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:39.110
you want to have a debate at
a university like Makerere,

00:38:40.230 --> 00:38:42.630
where maybe you are,

00:38:42.630 --> 00:38:44.730
there should be the freedom

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.370
for young people to debate these issues

00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:52.370
because it is from intellectual
centers like universities

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:57.480
that we can challenge
stigma and discrimination.

00:38:57.480 --> 00:39:01.320
I think education on human rights,

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:06.320
on women's rights and using the space,

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:11.390
the academic space to
vocalize, to debate to...

00:39:12.708 --> 00:39:16.020
I mean, why can't there be a pride

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:23.130
much at Makerere, if it
can't happen outside,

00:39:23.130 --> 00:39:26.283
within the gates of the
university, why can't it happen?

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:30.480
So we really need to
challenge young people

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:35.480
about human rights and women's rights.

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:40.290
I see young people and I get mad,

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:44.550
young people in my country, young men

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:46.470
who are little patriarchs

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:48.840
in the making who they already are.

00:39:48.840 --> 00:39:53.130
They've got an attitude about
being superior to women.

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:58.130
Is the education coming from
tradition, religion and so on?

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:02.550
We have to challenge it,
this is a equal world.

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:07.320
So I see the importance
of teaching human rights

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:12.060
to all students, science
students, art students,

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:14.763
and women's rights and debating,

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:18.240
this is what I can say about health,

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:22.803
because health is
ultimately a human right.

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:27.030
It is not just something to trade

00:40:27.030 --> 00:40:29.580
and to give away to reach people.

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:32.160
It's a human right and we must center it

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:36.393
around human rights and
focus on it as a human right.

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:41.520
- Thank you for those in inspiring words.

00:40:41.520 --> 00:40:44.910
We wanna now shift in our
last 20 or minutes so together

00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:47.730
to open up the questions
to some audience members.

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:50.340
And I'll start with one
from Edward Kabagande

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:53.310
who is asking about
cardiovascular diseases.

00:40:53.310 --> 00:40:56.550
As we all know, these are
emerging as comorbidities

00:40:56.550 --> 00:41:01.200
in HIV/AIDS patients as we've
been able to enable patients

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:03.720
to live longer with HIV/AIDS.

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:04.980
And the question is,

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:08.368
are there coordinated cardiovascular
disease control efforts

00:41:08.368 --> 00:41:11.850
in especially thinking about
low and middle income countries

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:15.240
for preventing cardiovascular
disease in HIV populations?

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:19.530
I think specifically Edward
Edmund is interested in

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:21.183
high blood pressure and stroke.

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.690
- What is the question?

00:41:24.690 --> 00:41:27.540
- Are there coordinated
cardiovascular disease

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:30.600
control efforts that are happening

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:33.540
to prevent cardiovascular
disease in HIV populations?

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:35.340
I know there are some activities

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:39.330
that are going on among
faculty members here at Yale,

00:41:39.330 --> 00:41:41.910
but more broadly, what is your perspective

00:41:41.910 --> 00:41:44.670
on integrating care for chronic diseases,

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:46.680
especially cardiovascular diseases

00:41:46.680 --> 00:41:48.093
in people living with HIV?

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:54.000
- I think as the years go,

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:58.953
and we are now 40 years
since HIV was first isolated,

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:04.350
and as treatments improve and people live

00:42:04.350 --> 00:42:08.100
longer and longer lives with HIV,

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:12.870
the issues of comorbidities
have become very important

00:42:12.870 --> 00:42:15.400
that people living with HIV

00:42:16.301 --> 00:42:21.301
and how other diseases affect them,

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:24.543
including high blood pressure and others.

00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:30.470
This is becoming more and more
important in understanding.

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:35.820
Of course for us as a response,

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:38.760
a global response on HIV/AIDS,

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:43.760
the big ones that we pay
attention to are TB and HIV

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:48.360
cause it is a strong correlation.

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:53.360
The most AIDS related
deaths are deaths from TB.

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:58.740
And then now increasingly
we see that mental health

00:42:58.740 --> 00:43:01.050
is so important.

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:04.590
For many, many people
who live on treatment

00:43:04.590 --> 00:43:08.520
for a very long time it
affects their mental health.

00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:12.510
And this is something we
have not worked on for long.

00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:14.640
About high blood pressure,

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:16.170
I think that is probably

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:19.920
lower down the list of priorities

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:24.570
that we work on in the
global HIV response.

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:26.850
But it's probably an important area

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:29.673
and I have not read much about it.

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:34.650
- Well thanks for your thoughts there.

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:37.590
I'd also also to talk a little bit about,

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:39.780
we highlighted and you highlighted earlier

00:43:39.780 --> 00:43:43.200
this issue of adolescent
girls and young women,

00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:46.749
but women more generally
being much more vulnerable

00:43:46.749 --> 00:43:48.390
to the effects of HIV.

00:43:48.390 --> 00:43:51.180
And one of the questioners is asking,

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:54.030
just given this disproportionate impact,

00:43:54.030 --> 00:43:56.490
could you comment a
little bit on coordinated

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:58.740
and concentrated efforts
to tailor treatments

00:43:58.740 --> 00:44:00.720
specifically to women?

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:03.510
One example is given is
sort of breaking the cycle

00:44:03.510 --> 00:44:05.430
of maternal to child transmission,

00:44:05.430 --> 00:44:09.180
but I know there's many other
women specific examples,

00:44:09.180 --> 00:44:12.300
it'd be interesting to
hear your perspective on.

00:44:12.300 --> 00:44:17.300
- That takes me back also
to the social determinants.

00:44:17.640 --> 00:44:22.640
Do you know that it is a shame
that children are left behind

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:25.950
in the HIV response.

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:30.950
That globally 75% of all
people living with HIV,

00:44:31.017 --> 00:44:32.643
are on treatment,

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:38.670
but the number for children is just 50%.

00:44:38.670 --> 00:44:39.503
50%.

00:44:39.503 --> 00:44:43.350
So half the children living with HIV today

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:46.410
are not on treatment, they are dying.

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:48.660
And we still have countries,

00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:53.660
many countries even in spite
of the treatment that is there,

00:44:53.670 --> 00:44:58.670
that still have mothers
transmitting HIV to their babies.

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:02.103
And you have to ask yourself why.

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:05.310
Goes back to inequalities.

00:45:05.310 --> 00:45:07.380
When you look at those countries

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:12.380
that have high rates of
mother to child transmission,

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:19.050
they're the same countries

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:23.327
or those countries have a high...

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:27.990
Or let me put it this with yeah,

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:32.913
high numbers of teenage mothers.

00:45:33.875 --> 00:45:35.790
Teenage mothers.

00:45:35.790 --> 00:45:40.140
You find that the biggest
number of the mothers

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:44.250
who have transmitted HIV to their babies

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:46.473
are actually children themselves.

00:45:47.460 --> 00:45:50.070
They are girls who've given birth.

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:55.070
They are 16, they're 17,
they're 18, and this is...

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:59.703
And they're poor and this is
the reason why they transmit.

00:46:00.750 --> 00:46:04.980
First as you know, if
you are living in Africa,

00:46:04.980 --> 00:46:06.993
if a girl gets pregnant,

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:12.330
perhaps seven out of 10 chances are that

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:13.743
she will leave home.

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:17.940
She will go stay with an aunt.

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:19.920
She'll go stay with her sister,

00:46:19.920 --> 00:46:22.530
she'll go to hide at her boyfriends.

00:46:22.530 --> 00:46:26.430
She's shunned by the
family and the community.

00:46:26.430 --> 00:46:30.960
That movement alone means
that she moves away from

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:35.073
where she has services, where
she knows, she's in hiding.

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:40.940
Those are factors that lead
to not accessing services.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.990
'Cause if she went for antenatal,

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:47.430
she would quickly tested
and put on treatment

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:49.110
and she wouldn't transmit.

00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:51.810
So we have to solve many issues,

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:56.760
not enough to put medicine in the clinics,

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.320
ARVs in the clinics.

00:46:58.320 --> 00:47:02.190
We have to solve those
issues of teenage mothers.

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:06.090
And when we don't connect
these two, we keep saying,

00:47:06.090 --> 00:47:08.373
but why are the numbers coming down?

00:47:09.240 --> 00:47:12.960
We have some programs,
I've seen some programs

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:17.223
where they tell a mother to bring,

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:20.463
bring the man who made you pregnant.

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:23.160
They disappear, they don't come back

00:47:24.455 --> 00:47:27.360
because the circumstances are that

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:31.620
she dare not even tell
whoever made her pregnant

00:47:31.620 --> 00:47:34.410
that she's HIV positive.

00:47:34.410 --> 00:47:36.990
So addressing those social factors,

00:47:36.990 --> 00:47:40.620
addressing those barriers
that stop young women

00:47:40.620 --> 00:47:44.850
from coming forward to save
their babies is important.

00:47:44.850 --> 00:47:47.403
And once the baby's born positive,

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:51.540
they also fear the stigma

00:47:51.540 --> 00:47:54.160
of taking the child to be put on treatment

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:56.760
because then the child will know

00:47:56.760 --> 00:47:58.620
and then her secret will be known.

00:47:58.620 --> 00:48:01.140
And I've just come from Jamaica.

00:48:01.140 --> 00:48:04.710
I went to a community led service,

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:07.890
I met five users of the service.

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:11.460
Two of them were women who
had been using the center

00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:15.900
for 18 years, they'd been
going there for their ARVs.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.240
They laughed, they
joked, they were at home

00:48:18.240 --> 00:48:22.680
with the leaders of the
center, the service providers.

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:27.600
When we talked, I asked them,
I said, they had children.

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:32.017
I said, "Do you tell young
women in your community

00:48:32.017 --> 00:48:37.017
"to be careful, to save
themselves, to prevent,

00:48:38.677 --> 00:48:40.567
"to ask for safe sex?

00:48:40.567 --> 00:48:42.270
"Do you tell them?"

00:48:42.270 --> 00:48:47.137
They said, "What! We
cannot say the word HIV.

00:48:48.037 --> 00:48:52.447
"Nobody in our community knows
that we are HIV positive.

00:48:52.447 --> 00:48:54.217
"If we talked about HIV,

00:48:54.217 --> 00:48:56.940
"we would be known and
we would be shunned."

00:48:56.940 --> 00:49:01.940
Imagine 18 years, their
communities know nothing.

00:49:03.672 --> 00:49:06.510
Their friends who know their secret

00:49:06.510 --> 00:49:09.000
are those who come to the center.

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:13.500
So imagine this kind of stigma,

00:49:13.500 --> 00:49:18.240
what that means for a
child growing with HIV

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:20.490
who is HIV positive.

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:21.810
It's too hard.

00:49:21.810 --> 00:49:24.387
So many moms will not tell their child

00:49:24.387 --> 00:49:26.640
and will not let them have treatment

00:49:26.640 --> 00:49:28.023
and will watch them die.

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:34.340
So we have to address those
issues of stigma, fight stigma,

00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:41.730
fight discrimination
before we can have everyone

00:49:41.730 --> 00:49:43.130
come forward to get service.

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:47.969
- Winnie, there's a lot of interest

00:49:47.969 --> 00:49:49.860
in sort of the effects of COVID-19.

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:53.400
Some people asking about
whether it affects HIV outcomes

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:56.370
and what's the burden of
COVID-19 on people with HIV.

00:49:56.370 --> 00:49:58.080
But maybe more broadly,

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:00.930
quite a few questions about how COVID-19

00:50:00.930 --> 00:50:03.420
has affected the way we
deliver HIV services.

00:50:03.420 --> 00:50:05.820
Whether you're thinking about HIV testing

00:50:05.820 --> 00:50:09.045
or even some of the social factors.

00:50:09.045 --> 00:50:12.510
Some are highlighting just
the role of domestic violence

00:50:12.510 --> 00:50:14.910
kind of during the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:17.250
Maybe the broad question would be then is,

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:21.870
how has COVID-19 changed
the task that you faced

00:50:21.870 --> 00:50:24.750
and that we all face to
kind of address HIV/AIDS

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:26.730
going forward the pandemic.

00:50:26.730 --> 00:50:31.730
- Let me start with what is
positive, that excites me.

00:50:31.920 --> 00:50:36.120
Some things changed for
the better from COVID

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:38.610
in our HIV response.

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:42.507
Some policies, for example,
that we had been advocating

00:50:42.507 --> 00:50:47.507
and that we know would help
people to stay on treatment

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:53.720
and be safe and be virally
suppressed finally came to happen

00:50:56.220 --> 00:50:59.190
like multi-month dispensing.

00:50:59.190 --> 00:51:02.130
We had been telling many governments that,

00:51:02.130 --> 00:51:05.730
please allow people who are regular

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:10.440
in coming for their treatment
to get a stock of drugs

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:14.070
for six months or at least
start with three months

00:51:14.070 --> 00:51:16.350
so they don't have to come every month.

00:51:16.350 --> 00:51:19.050
But many governments fearing

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:20.970
that they are not in good control

00:51:20.970 --> 00:51:23.853
of the supply chain wouldn't do it.

00:51:25.230 --> 00:51:28.500
So they were still requiring
people to come every month,

00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:31.350
sometimes every twice a month.

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:36.350
But when COVID hit and
the public safety measures

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:40.110
required to lock down people,

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:41.850
then they had no choice

00:51:41.850 --> 00:51:45.240
but start rolling out
multi-month dispensing.

00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:47.940
And then they realized that it works,

00:51:47.940 --> 00:51:51.030
that it actually saves them.

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:54.450
Because people were not
coming to the clinic.

00:51:54.450 --> 00:51:57.660
Then the second other innovations came in.

00:51:57.660 --> 00:52:01.650
You got people, I'm always
proud to tell this story.

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:06.650
In India, people living with
HIV, getting on their bikes,

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:13.080
collecting the ARVs for a group of them

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:16.320
and going around
distributing to each other,

00:52:16.320 --> 00:52:18.491
so they would take turns.

00:52:18.491 --> 00:52:20.160
These were innovations.

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:22.383
And then using the phone,

00:52:23.610 --> 00:52:27.220
finally telemedicine came to health to HIV

00:52:28.080 --> 00:52:32.340
because now they were using
the phone to locate people,

00:52:32.340 --> 00:52:35.700
to drop off supplies
because people fear stigma.

00:52:35.700 --> 00:52:38.490
They tell you where to
drop it, they pick it up,

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:40.410
but it's all on the phone.

00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:42.930
So all these innovations came

00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:45.180
in the way services are provided

00:52:45.180 --> 00:52:49.050
as a result of the public safety measures.

00:52:49.050 --> 00:52:51.513
But bad things also happened.

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:53.940
Violations of human rights.

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:55.800
People who are already stigmatized

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:59.460
tend to be targeted, scapegoated

00:52:59.460 --> 00:53:03.060
when there's fear and
panicking in society.

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:06.840
So you got gay men

00:53:06.840 --> 00:53:11.790
suddenly becoming more
oppressed, more victimized.

00:53:11.790 --> 00:53:15.450
You got sex workers being
thrown out of their homes

00:53:15.450 --> 00:53:18.120
and being told that they are victors

00:53:18.120 --> 00:53:21.840
of the coronavirus.

00:53:21.840 --> 00:53:25.920
I came to Uganda to see President Museveni

00:53:25.920 --> 00:53:30.920
because there was a group
of about 40 or so gay men

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:34.080
who were found hiding in
a shelter and arrested

00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:37.050
and taken to prison for nothing.

00:53:37.050 --> 00:53:40.623
And horrible tests were done on them.

00:53:41.558 --> 00:53:46.558
So I went to see him and asked
him to have them released.

00:53:48.720 --> 00:53:52.710
Then he said to me, I need
to tell you this anecdote.

00:53:52.710 --> 00:53:57.710
He said, "But they are in prison

00:53:57.907 --> 00:54:02.907
"because they violated COVID
rules or COVID laws here.

00:54:03.127 --> 00:54:06.957
"There are not supposed to
be so many in one building."

00:54:08.040 --> 00:54:09.170
Can you not...

00:54:10.080 --> 00:54:12.337
I said, "But it's a
shelter, Mr. President,

00:54:12.337 --> 00:54:14.527
"these are people run
away from their homes."

00:54:14.527 --> 00:54:16.037
"They're in a shelter."

00:54:16.037 --> 00:54:19.077
And said, "Well, can't you
UNAIDS build many houses?"

00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:21.330
What kind of question is that?

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:24.517
I said, "But we don't
build houses for people.

00:54:24.517 --> 00:54:28.927
"This is a kind person
who's given them a shelter

00:54:28.927 --> 00:54:30.567
"and that's where they stay."

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:33.937
And he said, "Well, we're going to see,

00:54:33.937 --> 00:54:36.607
"but this is a breach of our rules,

00:54:36.607 --> 00:54:39.303
"we don't want many people crowding.

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:41.677
Then I looked at him, I said,

00:54:41.677 --> 00:54:46.410
"Mr. President," he was in
his state house in Entebbe.

00:54:46.410 --> 00:54:47.287
I said, Mr. President,

00:54:47.287 --> 00:54:50.013
"but down the road there on Buganda Road,

00:54:50.887 --> 00:54:53.647
"there's a group of 50 women in one house,

00:54:53.647 --> 00:54:55.437
"why haven't you arrested them?"

00:54:56.617 --> 00:54:58.800
He said, "What! Who are those?"

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:01.527
I said, "The nuns, they're in a convent."

00:55:02.700 --> 00:55:04.140
And he busted out and laughed.

00:55:04.140 --> 00:55:05.487
He said, "You go to me."

00:55:07.366 --> 00:55:10.170
But it's totally ridiculous

00:55:10.170 --> 00:55:15.170
that gay men staying in
a shelter were a risk

00:55:15.330 --> 00:55:17.913
but in all these other places,

00:55:19.140 --> 00:55:23.160
they were religious communities
nobody was touching them.

00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:26.760
So discrimination was rampant.

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:30.660
And we saw the violation of human rights.

00:55:30.660 --> 00:55:35.100
We issued a report about
it, drew attention to it.

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:36.090
So those were the...

00:55:36.090 --> 00:55:39.870
But in terms of the general
response to HIV/AIDS

00:55:39.870 --> 00:55:44.870
some things also hurt
us, were thrown off track

00:55:45.030 --> 00:55:49.110
because governments run out of money.

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:51.540
Both the donors were pouring money

00:55:51.540 --> 00:55:53.890
into saving their people

00:55:54.840 --> 00:55:57.690
and were putting in their economies

00:55:57.690 --> 00:56:01.740
to keep them growing or at least stable.

00:56:01.740 --> 00:56:03.570
And we are cutting the money,

00:56:03.570 --> 00:56:08.570
the little money that goes
to supporting HIV responses

00:56:08.820 --> 00:56:10.050
in developing countries.

00:56:10.050 --> 00:56:12.630
We fought to keep this money flowing

00:56:12.630 --> 00:56:14.580
and we are still fighting.

00:56:14.580 --> 00:56:18.180
But we were hurt because many countries

00:56:18.180 --> 00:56:23.180
still depend on the
American program, PEPFA

00:56:23.490 --> 00:56:25.620
as well as the global fund.

00:56:25.620 --> 00:56:27.390
And we were seeing...

00:56:27.390 --> 00:56:29.460
But actually I have to say this credit

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:31.320
to the United States government.

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:32.970
In the middle of all that,

00:56:32.970 --> 00:56:36.240
they actually increased their contribution

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:41.240
to some of our work, but
some other countries reduced.

00:56:41.520 --> 00:56:46.380
We also saw, sadly that the same story

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:49.740
of what had happened with ARVs

00:56:49.740 --> 00:56:51.940
when they were first produced

00:56:52.950 --> 00:56:57.950
and it took 10 years before
they became available

00:56:58.530 --> 00:57:01.260
in the south, because of the price,

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:04.110
because of the monopoly of companies.

00:57:04.110 --> 00:57:05.700
We saw it play out again.

00:57:05.700 --> 00:57:09.780
And we had tried to say,
let's learn the best lesson.

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:12.450
12 million people died mostly in Africa

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:15.810
while we waited for
the price to come down.

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:17.940
Does it have to happen again?

00:57:17.940 --> 00:57:20.970
But no, we didn't learn the lesson.

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:24.540
So again, we saw the same rules

00:57:24.540 --> 00:57:27.663
working to hurt a global response.

00:57:28.710 --> 00:57:31.770
But now we came back fighting.

00:57:31.770 --> 00:57:35.580
We are now fighting for
long acting injectables

00:57:35.580 --> 00:57:40.560
Long acting injectables for
prevention are now available.

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:43.560
If you are in New York, you can have it.

00:57:43.560 --> 00:57:48.420
You can get a jab and for
two months you are safe.

00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:50.940
You can have sex and you will not,

00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:54.723
with someone who has HIV,
but you won't contract it.

00:57:55.650 --> 00:57:58.020
If you are in London, you can have it.

00:57:58.020 --> 00:57:59.820
It's affordable,

00:57:59.820 --> 00:58:04.820
but it's not available for a
gay man yet in South Africa

00:58:05.310 --> 00:58:07.770
or in Uganda or in Kenya,

00:58:07.770 --> 00:58:09.870
the price is still too high

00:58:09.870 --> 00:58:12.900
and the countries cannot import it yet.

00:58:12.900 --> 00:58:15.303
We are fighting for
this price to come down.

00:58:16.505 --> 00:58:21.505
Long acting antiretrovirals
will be a game changer

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:24.990
for young women and girls.

00:58:24.990 --> 00:58:29.990
Because young girls and young women are,

00:58:30.030 --> 00:58:34.200
most of the time, hiding,
don't want to be known that

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:35.850
either they're having sex

00:58:35.850 --> 00:58:40.020
or they're positive and can't control,

00:58:41.790 --> 00:58:44.340
can't negotiate safe sex.

00:58:44.340 --> 00:58:47.250
But imagine having an injection

00:58:47.250 --> 00:58:52.250
that prevents for six
months, or you have the ARV

00:58:54.120 --> 00:58:55.920
and you don't have to swallow a tablet,

00:58:55.920 --> 00:58:58.740
nobody has to see if you are a girl,

00:58:58.740 --> 00:59:00.270
you're not swallowing a tablet,

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:02.940
you're not causing attention to yourself

00:59:02.940 --> 00:59:05.070
but you get a jab every six months.

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:06.900
It'll be a game changer,

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:11.250
but it'll be available
first in rich countries,

00:59:11.250 --> 00:59:14.370
we'll have to fight to get the price down.

00:59:14.370 --> 00:59:17.940
Some people will die before
the price comes down,

00:59:17.940 --> 00:59:19.680
but we'll do it, we have to do it.

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:24.300
And you young people must
be part of these campaigns.

00:59:24.300 --> 00:59:29.300
And it was the alliance of
gay men in America and Europe

00:59:30.420 --> 00:59:31.890
with people in the south

00:59:31.890 --> 00:59:35.100
that brought the price
of antiretroviral down

00:59:35.100 --> 00:59:38.610
and the leadership of people
like President Mandela.

00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:43.610
So it'll take global
alliances to end AIDS,

00:59:45.180 --> 00:59:48.300
to bring prices down, to
have access to medicine,

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:50.580
to the best of science,

00:59:50.580 --> 00:59:53.370
and to remove these laws and policies

00:59:53.370 --> 00:59:56.223
that create barriers for people.

00:59:57.780 --> 00:59:59.250
- Winnie, thank you.

00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:02.370
This has been a remarkable conversation.

01:00:02.370 --> 01:00:05.370
I think we could easily
go for another hour.

01:00:05.370 --> 01:00:07.890
The audience has been
inspired by your comments,

01:00:07.890 --> 01:00:10.740
and I think you've given
both a general framework

01:00:10.740 --> 01:00:12.840
for thinking about where
we're falling short,

01:00:12.840 --> 01:00:17.250
namely addressing equality and
also given us room for hope

01:00:17.250 --> 01:00:20.250
that some of the victories
that have been one in the past

01:00:20.250 --> 01:00:22.380
for standing up for economic justice

01:00:22.380 --> 01:00:25.170
and standing up for those

01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:28.740
who are adversely affected
by stigma and discrimination

01:00:28.740 --> 01:00:29.880
that we can carry those forward

01:00:29.880 --> 01:00:32.850
with new technologies and do better.

01:00:32.850 --> 01:00:35.100
We thank you so much for joining us today,

01:00:35.100 --> 01:00:37.530
and we'll be following your work closely

01:00:37.530 --> 01:00:42.319
and we'll take forward your
words with inspiration.

01:00:42.319 --> 01:00:43.680
- Well, thank you so much

01:00:43.680 --> 01:00:47.163
and greetings to my brothers
and sisters in Uganda.

01:00:49.170 --> 01:00:51.300
Thank you for being with you

01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:56.300
and I enjoy engaging with
students and intellectuals.

01:00:56.610 --> 01:00:57.443
Thank you.

01:00:58.410 --> 01:00:59.510
- Thank you very much.

